FILED s543q (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-28, CODE 6a1301ed = MAIN-READY-114 + records, v2-16500) by the Rosetta census B (docs/rosetta-plan.md section 6b; #2104).

THE SHAPES (neutral reproducers, run on the tree, perl 5.40.3 vs PCL):

    use utf8;
    my $É = 1;
    $É++;
    my $é = 5;
    $é++;
    print "$É $é\n";
    perl: 2 6
    PCL : 2 5          (exit 0, nothing on stderr: the second increment went to another symbol)

    use utf8;
    my $é = 5;
    $é += 2;
    my $ü = 1;
    $ü = $ü + 1;
    my $ö = 1;
    $ö--;
    print "$é $ü $ö\n";
    perl: 7 2 0
    PCL : Unhandled unbound-variable ... The variable $<one byte> is unbound.   + a Lisp backtrace (exit 1; the name is printed
          as a single non-UTF-8 byte)

WHERE (emitted Lisp, the second shape): `(p-let ((|$é| :scalar 5)) ... (p-incf-raw $é 2 :numeric) ... (setf $ü (p-+ |$ü| 1))
... (p-decf-raw $ö :numeric) ...)` -- the binding and every READ are bar-quoted, the raw-slot WRITE forms (`p-incf-raw`,
`p-decf-raw`, the `setf` target) are not.  The runtime's readtable case is :invert (docs: [:invert case-sensitivity]), under
which an unescaped name whose letters are all one case reads INVERTED (`$é` -> `$É`, `$λ` -> `$Λ`), while `|$é|` keeps its
case: two symbols.  ASCII names are emitted unescaped at both sites, so they agree.  On 0313 the emission is
`(p-let ((|$λ| :scalar 1)) ... (setf $λ 0) ... (p-incf-raw $λ 1 :numeric) ...)`: the comparison reads `|$λ|`, which never
changes, so Brent's loop never ends.  One escaping function should decide the spelling for every site (rule 11).

CHEAP DISCRIMINATING MEASUREMENT (taken): 0313 with its two lexicals renamed to ASCII (`$lam`, `$mu`) is byte-identical to
perl (0.2 s) -- its only cause; 1497 is four lines and has no other statement.

PROGRAMS: 1497-Unicode-variable-names, 0313-Cycle-detection (class TIMEOUT: the endless loop).
